verdict.reason:
The code is not as described. `escapeMarkdownAlt` (packages/docs/src/serialize/markdown.ts:388-390) does not escape only `[\\]]`; it is a delegation `return escapeMarkdownText(alt);`. `escapeMarkdownText` (:371-375) folds `LINE_SEPARATORS` (:359, `/[
  ]/g`) to a space AND backslash-escapes `<` along with `\ * _ [ ] \` ~` via `/[\*_[\]`~<]/g`. Both legs of the claimed vector are therefore neutralized before `imageInline` emits `![${alt}](...)` at :341: the blank line cannot close the paragraph (newlines become spaces) and the `<` cannot open a tag (it is escaped). The finding appears to have read the doc comment at :377-386, which describes the old `]`-only behavior in the past tense as the thing being replaced, as if it were current code. The exact payload is regression-tested: markdown.test.ts:507-511 asserts the output of `alted('cap

not a new block')` matches no `[
]` at all, and :513-518 asserts `alted('<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>')` === `![\<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>](https://x/y.png)` with `not.toMatch(/(^|[^\])</)`. The finding's observation that `UNEMITTABLE_URL_CHARS` (:263, applied at :297 and :317) covers only the URL is factually true but does not establish the vulnerability, because alt is guarded by a different, present mechanism. The only other `![alt](...)` emitter, packages/notes/src/view/commands.ts:205, is in a different package with its own `escapeAlt` and is not the location named by this finding.
